    {
      "id": "claim_91ace76618fe484b8d16d321d5fed93d",
      "statement": "The Markov model is a back-off n-gram rather than a fixed-order one: it maintains a separate count table for every order up to its maximum and falls back from the longest available context to shorter ones when predicting.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://pipeline/model/typical_path/markov/markov_model.py#L10-L46",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:48b3b86c3de67891a8d2e8f0b87c90c996625465352d271eda09df6c07f38756:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_952c08f8c6b7454cac8ce2a4381cd9a2",
      "statement": "The directly-follows model subclasses the first-order Markov model and fits in two passes, first counting how many distinct traces contain each transition and keeping only those above a coverage floor, then counting transitions over the surviving edges alone — making it a frequency-filtered first-order chain and therefore a like-for-like baseline against the higher-order model.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://pipeline/model/typical_eval.py#L31-L31",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:43f1d6957737f62376b19605bda97545721fde68fdcb7c9497632a03d17bcf2f:"
        },
        {
          "resource": "repo://pipeline/model/typical_path/dfg/dfg_model.py",
          "version": "repo-file-v1:sha256:569f23ee5caa447034a2222ca7521a9e2219d772c953dc9f9f7004b6ce1f5988"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "claim_d275f4ae91d640c787e88f69d332a8bf",
      "statement": "The three neural sequence models share a common torch base that reserves padding and unknown at vocabulary indices zero and one, encodes a fixed-size left-padded window of recent states, and resolves its device through the shared device selector.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "resource": "repo://pipeline/model/typical_path/_torch_base.py#L8-L47",
          "version": "repo-lines-v1:sha256:3390dd9fd9466ccfb82aad5f8922420b1fbe946026a2ffc0d4260c0fcab722c5:"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
